  {
   "thing": {
    "kind": "text",
    "text": "ACTIVITY 5\nRationals greater than 1\n\nPat says there must be more\nnumbers above 1 than between\n0 and 1 -- after all, above 1\nthere is endless room.\n\nYou could redo Activity 4 with\nthe scale the other way round.\nOr you could notice that every\na/b below 1 has a b/a above\nit, and just turn the sequence\nover.\n\nDivides 1 is three steps long:\ntake a 1, drop the incoming\nnumber on it set to DIVIDE,\nhand out the result.\n\nThe pairing comes free. 1/2\ndances with 2, 2/3 with 3/2."
   },
   "x": -1.25,
   "z": 2.4
  },
  {
   "thing": {
    "kind": "text",
    "text": "TO RUN IT\n\nPull the lever on the All\nFractions room \u2014 and pull it\nagain when you have seen\nenough.\n\nThree rooms run at once: the\nboxes become fractions, and\nthe fractions become their own\nreciprocals.\n\nSally objects: there is a whole\ncopy of 0-to-1 between 1 and 2,\nanother between 2 and 3, and so\non forever -- so surely there\nare infinitely many times more\nabove 1?\n\nEvery number on the right nest\ncame from exactly one on the\nleft. What is wrong with\nSally's argument?"
   },
   "x": 0.3,
   "z": 2.9
  }
